--- title
# Chris Cleaner

--- body
Disk analysis and file cataloging with native system integration and background processing.

--- actions
[Product website](https://christiansoftware.org/) · [All products](/en/#products)
:::

::: section topic-1
## Scanning at scale

A disk catalog is built from a large number of filesystem entries whose metadata is not always cheap to collect. Chris Cleaner separates enumeration from the interface, allowing information to arrive incrementally instead of waiting for an entire volume to be scanned.

Bounded concurrent work is useful here: producers cannot fill memory indefinitely when indexing or presentation is slower than file discovery.
:::

::: section topic-2
## Native capabilities

The application uses .NET and a desktop interface, with system-specific adapters for operations that cannot be expressed through a generic cross-platform API. On Windows, filesystem facilities support incremental observation and catalog maintenance.

This leaves an explicit portability boundary. A feature available on one filesystem or operating system may require a different implementation—or a simpler fallback—elsewhere.
:::

::: section topic-3
## Cleanup is a separate responsibility

Finding a file and deciding to remove it are not the same operation. Disk analysis should remain useful independently of cleanup. Any destructive workflow needs clear user review and conservative defaults.

The code includes concurrent scanning and catalog-related components. Repeatable performance measurements and comprehensive deletion-safety tests were not established during the review, so no quantitative speed or safety claim is made.
:::

